    <monitor x="718" precision="0" y="65" height="70" fontSize="12" width="126" display="Unaware">unaware-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="858" precision="0" y="65" height="70" fontSize="12" width="126" display="Believers">believer-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="998" precision="0" y="65" height="70" fontSize="12" width="126" display="Verified">verified-count</monitor>
    <note x="718" y="143" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="11" width="385" markdown="false" height="28" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Agent populations over time</note>
    <plot x="718" autoPlotX="true" yMax="200.0" autoPlotY="true" yAxis="Students" y="172" xMin="0.0" height="310" legend="true" xMax="100.0" yMin="0.0" width="410" xAxis="Ticks" display="Rumour Spread Over Time">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Unaware" color="-11908395" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot unaware-count</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Believers" color="-2448096" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot believer-count</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Verified" color="-13996368" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot verified-count</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <note x="718" y="489" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="10" width="390" markdown="false" height="25" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">BLUE = UNAWARE   |   ORANGE = BELIEVER   |   GREEN = VERIFIED</note>
  </widgets>
  <info>## WHAT IS IT?

Campus Rumour Spreading Simulation is an educational agent-based model showing how a rumour can propagate through local interactions among moving students, and how individual fact-checking affects that propagation.

Each student is an autonomous "turtle" (agent). The model is conceptual: it is not a prediction of actual campus behaviour.

## HOW IT WORKS

- Unaware (sky blue): the student has not adopted the rumour.
- Believer (orange): the student has adopted the rumour and can spread it.
- Verified (green): the student has fact-checked and no longer spreads it.

1. SETUP creates the student population at random positions and selects the initial believers.
2. On each tick, students move with random turns and a forward step of 0.5 units.
3. Existing believers attempt to spread the rumour to unaware students within a radius of 1.5 units. Each eligible interaction succeeds with SPREAD-PROBABILITY percent chance.
4. All believers then independently fact-check with FACT-CHECK-PROBABILITY percent chance. Fact-checked students turn green and no longer spread the rumour.
5. The simulation stops if there are no believers or if MAX-TICKS is reached.

The model evaluates spreading before fact-checking each tick. Thus a student can spread during a tick and then fact-check during that same tick. Students newly converted during a spreading phase may fact-check during that tick; they first become potential spreaders in the next spreading phase.

## HOW TO USE IT

- POPULATION: total number of students.
- INITIAL-BELIEVERS: number who start believing the rumour.
- SPREAD-PROBABILITY: chance (%) of conversion per eligible local encounter.
- FACT-CHECK-PROBABILITY: chance (%) for each believer to stop spreading per tick.
- MAX-TICKS: maximum time steps to run.
- SETUP: reset and initialize the model.
- GO: run the model until stopped; click again to stop manually.

The right-hand monitors display counts of unaware, believing and verified students. The chart tracks these counts over time.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

- The three counts always add up to POPULATION.
- Because chance and movement affect interactions, identical settings can lead to different outcomes.
- Increasing fact-checking can reduce the number of current spreaders, but its effect depends on the other parameters.
- With spreading set to 0% and fact-checking set to 0%, the simulation reaches the MAX-TICKS limit without any state changes.

## THINGS TO TRY

Try setting POPULATION = 200, INITIAL-BELIEVERS = 4, and MAX-TICKS = 300. Repeat each run several times.

1. High-spread scenario: SPREAD-PROBABILITY = 80, FACT-CHECK-PROBABILITY = 5.
2. Balanced scenario: SPREAD-PROBABILITY = 40, FACT-CHECK-PROBABILITY = 40.
3. Fact-checking scenario: SPREAD-PROBABILITY = 20, FACT-CHECK-PROBABILITY = 80.

Record the final counts and the stopping tick for every run. Compare repetitions rather than judging from a single random run.

## EXTENDING THE MODEL

Possible extensions include friend-based contact networks, varying levels of trust, different speeds, limited-time belief persistence, competing rumours, and CSV export of aggregated experimental results.

## MODEL LIMITATIONS

- Students interact only through spatial proximity, not real friendship networks or online messages.
- All agents share the same transmission and fact-checking probabilities.
- "Verified" means that an agent has stopped spreading after fact-checking; the model does not determine whether the rumour is factually true or false.
- The model makes no calibrated real-world forecasts.
- Transmission is attempted in NetLogo's agent execution order, not by fully simultaneous updates.

## NETLOGO FEATURES

The model uses turtles-own variables, agentsets, n-of, ask, in-radius, random-float, reporters, sliders, monitors, multi-pen plotting, and ticks.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Campus Rumour Spreading Simulation — academic Agent-Based Modelling project.
Repository: https://github.com/nikhil23maurya/campus-rumour-spreading-simulation
Platform: NetLogo (https://www.netlogo.org/)
</info>
